//! lotus/stereo_chorus.rye — ALES179: the CHORUS carried into stereo — ALES71's triangle-LFO modulated delay mixed DRY-PLUS-WET run on BOTH channels with the SAME LFO and the SAME mix (centre, depth, period, num/den), each channel
//! read off ITS OWN frozen snapshot, the fourth rung of the stereo MODULATION class and the vibrato's PARENT. ALES178 carried the vibrato — the modulated delay heard WET-ONLY — into stereo, the first delay-line stereo lift. The vibrato
//! is exactly ALES71's chorus with the dry removed and the wet at full: chorus[i] = saturate(dry[i] + vibrato[i]). Carrying the chorus itself into stereo is the natural parent rung — the same shared LFO on both channels, each on its own
//! snapshot, but now the delayed voice is MIXED UNDER THE DRY at a named level rather than heard alone. That mix returns the one fault the vibrato dropped: BadLevel (a zero denominator or a wet above unity), so StereoChorusError carries
//! five faults where the vibrato carried four. The chorus is what a keeper actually reaches for — one voice made to sound like several, the width a mix wants on a thin vocal or a lone guitar.
//!
//! The crux — the SAME LFO and mix on BOTH channels, each on its OWN snapshot, is the linked stereo chorus: both voices widen in lockstep, the image held. A chorus's centre, depth, period, and level are all scaled units or fractions
//! the caller NAMES, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free — no linking machinery, no measurement. Mono `chorus` snapshots the dry prefix of its OWN clip before
//! any write; run once per channel, the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed — NO cross-channel snapshot, the two channels never read each other. Unlike the wet-only vibrato, the chorus write is
//! saturate(dry[i] + level·wet[i]), so a loud dry-plus-wet sum clamps to the i16 rail per channel rather than wrapping. Because the read and the mix are LINEAR in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master stays
//! identical and a PANNED master (values kept off the rail) keeps its pan: both sides read at the same fractional delay off proportional snapshots and mixed at the same level, so the left:right ratio at every sample is untouched.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadPeriod (a period not a positive multiple of four, or wider than max_clip), BadDepth (a negative depth, or a swing dipping below one whole sample), BadDelay (a swing top past max_clip), BadLevel (a
//! zero denominator, or a wet above unity), and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE either channel is snapshotted — the LFO, mix, and span validated once against the shared length up front (ALES71's own `precheck`, factored
//! out for this exactly as ALES72's and ALES75's were), so a refusal never widens one channel and leaves the other torn; then run ALES71's `chorus` on each channel with the SAME LFO and mix.
//!
//! The crux — five laws. THE STEREO CHORUS LAW: each channel equals ALES71's mono `chorus` with the SAME LFO and mix over the same span byte for byte, proven side by side with genuinely different per-channel content — the left rung by
//! the left's samples, the right by the right's, never crossed. THE VIBRATO-FAMILY LAW: a full-wet (1/1) stereo chorus equals the dry master plus ALES178's stereo vibrato sample for sample on both channels (stereo_chorus[i] =
//! saturate(dry[i] + stereo_vibrato[i])), so the parent and the wet-only child are one delay heard two ways in stereo. THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW: a zero-level (num = 0) stereo chorus is the exact dry master on both channels — no wet added,
//! the identity — so the mix genuinely governs the wet. THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master stays identical through the chorus, AND a panned pair (values off the rail) keeps its left:right ratio at every sample. THE BALANCE /
//! SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an all-silent master widens to silence on both; left.len == right.len after; a bad period, a bad depth, a bad delay top, a bad level, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH
//! channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES179.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-083932_fill-ales179-lotus-stereo-chorus.md
//! Stands on: lotus/chorus.rye (ALES71) — the mono chorus run once per channel, its ChorusError reused whole and its `precheck` (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation; lotus/vibrato.rye (ALES72) — the wet-only
//! child, carried into stereo at ALES178, the vibrato-family equality target; lotus/glide.rye (ALES70) — glide_scale, the fixed-point unit (256 = unity); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip and its balance invariant; lotus/stereo_vibrato.rye
//! (ALES178) — the stereo wet-only voice this parent adds under the dry; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and the one true saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each rung through ALES71's own modulated delay — the triangle and the fractional interpolation in i64, each channel snapshotting its own dry
//! prefix into one bounded [max_clip]i16 scratch, the dry-plus-wet sum saturating once, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The LFO is counted in scaled units (256 = unity)
//! and the period in samples, not hertz; the mix is a named fraction, not a decibel. No cross-channel snapshot, no feedback (the flanger's element, the next rung), no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no
//! real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_chorus.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const glide = @import("glide.rye");
const chorus = @import("chorus.rye");
const stereo_vibrato = @import("stereo_vibrato.rye");

pub const stereo_chorus_version = "20260815.083932";

/// The stereo chorus's faults are exactly ALES71's own `ChorusError` — `BadPeriod`, `BadDepth`, `BadDelay`, `BadLevel`, and `BadRange` — reused by name, since carrying the chorus into stereo adds no new fault: it validates one shared
/// LFO, mix, and span against the shared length, then runs the same mono chorus on each channel. BadLevel is the one fault the wet-only stereo vibrato dropped, returned here by the dry-plus-wet mix.
pub const StereoChorusError = chorus.ChorusError;

/// Apply a chorus over `count` samples starting at `start` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the SAME LFO and mix — a triangle of `depth_scaled` round `centre_scaled` over `period` samples driving a modulated fractional delay, mixed under
/// the dry at `num/den` — by running ALES71's proven mono `chorus` on each channel. The shared LFO and mix preserve the stereo image: an identical-channel master stays identical, and a panned master keeps its pan (both sides read at the
/// same fractional delay off proportional snapshots and mixed at the same level, the read and mix linear in the input away from saturation). Each channel reads its OWN frozen snapshot — no cross-channel read. Validates the LFO, mix, and
/// span ONCE against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (ALES71's `precheck`): refuses BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadLevel, and BadRange, both channels untouched. Values only — never the length; the two channels
/// leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_chorus(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    centre_scaled: i64,
    depth_scaled: i64,
    period: u32,
    num: u32,
    den: u32,
) StereoChorusError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared LFO and mix preserve.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the LFO, mix, and span are legal against the SHARED length — checked before any snapshot, so a refusal never widens one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    try chorus.precheck(len, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, num, den);

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels (same LFO, same mix, same span, shared length), so each mono chorus is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME LFO and mix
    // on each channel, each reading its OWN frozen dry snapshot, never the other's.
    try chorus.chorus(&sc.left, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, num, den);
    try chorus.chorus(&sc.right, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, num, den);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a chorus writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO CHORUS LAW — each channel equals ALES71's mono chorus with the SAME LFO and mix byte for byte, different per-channel content; THE VIBRATO-FAMILY LAW — a full-wet stereo chorus equals dry plus ALES178's stereo
//     vibrato per channel; THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW — a zero-level chorus is the dry master on both; THE IMAGE LAW — an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its ratio; THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY /
//     DEGENERATE LAW — silence to silence, balanced, each of five refusals untouched, count 0 identity

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const scale = glide.glide_scale;

    // 1. THE STEREO CHORUS LAW — each channel equals ALES71's mono chorus with the SAME LFO and mix, proven side by side over one span with genuinely different per-channel content: the left rung by the left's samples, the right by the
    //    right's, never crossed.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 1000, -2000, 3000, -4000, 5000, -6000, 7000, -8000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_chorus(&sc, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &ll);
        try chorus.chorus(&ml, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &rr);
        try chorus.chorus(&mr, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChorusNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChorusNotMono;
        // The two channels genuinely differ — the left is not the right rung, proving no crossing.
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.ChannelsDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 2. THE VIBRATO-FAMILY LAW — a full-wet (1/1) stereo chorus equals the dry master plus ALES178's stereo vibrato, sample for sample on BOTH channels: stereo_chorus[i] = saturate(dry[i] + stereo_vibrato[i]).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 1000, -2000, 3000, -4000, 5000, -6000, 7000, -8000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000, 300, -300 };

        var full = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&full, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_chorus(&full, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 1); // full wet

        var wet = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&wet, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_vibrato.stereo_vibrato(&wet, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8);

        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 8) : (i += 1) {
            const want_l: i16 = timeline.saturate(@as(i64, ll[i]) + @as(i64, wet.left.samples()[i]));
            const want_r: i16 = timeline.saturate(@as(i64, rr[i]) + @as(i64, wet.right.samples()[i]));
            if (full.left.samples()[i] != want_l) return error.LeftChorusNotDryPlusVibrato;
            if (full.right.samples()[i] != want_r) return error.RightChorusNotDryPlusVibrato;
        }
    }

    // 3. THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW — a zero-level (num = 0) stereo chorus is the exact dry master on both channels: no wet added, the identity.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000, 300, -300 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_chorus(&sc, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 0, 1); // zero level — dry only
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll)) return error.ZeroLevelLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.ZeroLevelRightChanged;
    }

    // 4. THE IMAGE LAW — an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical through the chorus.
    {
        const same = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 5000, -400, 3000, -3000, 300, -300 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &same, &same);
        try stereo_chorus(&sc, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 4b. THE IMAGE LAW, panned — a panned pair (the right exactly half the left) keeps its left:right ratio at every sample: the read and the mix are LINEAR in the input, so both channels stay in ratio. The values are kept SMALL so the
    //     dry-plus-wet sum never touches the rail (where saturation would break a ratio), and the left values are even so the halving is exact.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 2000, -2000, 3000, -1000, 4000, -4000, 500, -500 };
        var rr: [8]i16 = undefined;
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 8) : (j += 1) rr[j] = @intCast(@divTrunc(@as(i64, ll[j]), 2)); // right is exactly half the left — a pan
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_chorus(&sc, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);
        const lo = sc.left.samples();
        const ro = sc.right.samples();
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < 8) : (k += 1) if (2 * @as(i64, ro[k]) != @as(i64, lo[k])) return error.PanRatioWandered;
    }

    // 5a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — an all-silent master widens to silence on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_chorus(&sc, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000, 3000, -3000, 100, -100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, 600, -600, 800, -800 });
        try stereo_chorus(&bc, 0, 8, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 1, 2);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 8) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 5b. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a bad period (not a multiple of four), a bad depth (a swing dipping below one whole sample), a bad delay top (a swing past max_clip), a bad level (a wet above unity), and an out-of-range span each refuse by
    //     name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7, -8 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5, 4 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_chorus(&a, 0, 4, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 6, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongBadPeriod; // period 6 not a multiple of four
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.PeriodRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_chorus(&b, 0, 4, 1 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadDepthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongBadDepth; // centre − depth = 0 < glide_scale
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.DepthRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        const too_far: i64 = @as(i64, timeline.max_clip) * scale + scale; // swing top whole delay > max_clip
        if (stereo_chorus(&c, 0, 4, too_far, 0, 4, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadDelaySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDelay) return error.WrongBadDelay;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.DelayRefusalMutated;

        var l = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&l, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_chorus(&l, 0, 4, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 5, 4)) |_| return error.BadLevelSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadLevel) return error.WrongBadLevel; // num 5 > den 4 — wet above unity
        if (!eq(l.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(l.right.samples(), &tr) or l.left.len != l.right.len) return error.LevelRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_chorus(&d, 1, 4, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange; // start 1 + count 4 > len 4
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.RangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 5c. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no sample to widen.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_chorus(&noop, 1, 0, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 2); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-chorus: the CHORUS CARRIED INTO STEREO — ALES71's triangle-LFO modulated delay mixed DRY-PLUS-WET run on BOTH channels with the SAME LFO and mix (centre, depth, period, num/den), each channel read off ITS " ++
        "OWN frozen snapshot, the fourth rung of the stereo MODULATION class and the vibrato's PARENT. ALES178 carried the vibrato (the same delay heard WET-ONLY) into stereo; the chorus is that delay mixed UNDER THE DRY at a named " ++
        "level, so it returns the one fault the vibrato dropped — BadLevel (a zero denominator or a wet above unity) — and StereoChorusError carries five faults where the vibrato carried four. The SAME LFO and mix on both channels, " ++
        "each on its OWN snapshot, is the linked stereo chorus: both voices widen in lockstep, the image held. The LFO and mix are scaled units and fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same " ++
        "parameters on each channel preserve the image for free; mono chorus snapshots the dry prefix of its OWN clip, so run once per channel the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed — no cross-channel " ++
        "snapshot. Unlike the wet-only vibrato, the write is saturate(dry + level·wet), so a loud sum clamps to the rail per channel; because the read and mix are linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master " ++
        "stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan. It validates the LFO, mix, and span once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadLevel, BadRange; ALES71's precheck " ++
        "reused), so a refusal never widens one channel and leaves the other torn; ChorusError reused whole. THE STEREO CHORUS LAW: each channel equals ALES71's mono chorus with the same LFO and mix byte for byte, the channels " ++
        "genuinely differing. THE VIBRATO-FAMILY LAW: a full-wet stereo chorus equals the dry master plus ALES178's stereo vibrato per channel, sample for sample. THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW: a zero-level chorus is the dry master on both. " ++
        "THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its left:right ratio. THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: silence to silence on both, left.len == right.len after, a bad " ++
        "period / a bad depth / a bad delay top / a bad level / an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips, each rung " ++
        "through the proven mono chorus's modulated delay, each channel snapshotting its own dry prefix and the dry-plus-wet sum saturating once, the LFO in scaled units not hertz and the mix a named fraction not a decibel; no " ++
        "cross-channel snapshot, no feedback, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
